WHAT: add a Drumstick RT backend pair, `wk-in` and `wk-out`, over the wk
      runtime's `wk:midi/midi` port, plus the `wk-common` glue that both halves
      share; build them on CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME=WASI; and give BackendManager a
      Q_OS_WASI arm so "wk" is the default backend name.
WHY:  this is the port. Everything else here is build plumbing; this patch is
      the reason the node can exchange MIDI with another node at all.

      Drumstick reaches MIDI hardware through plugins that implement MIDIInput
      and MIDIOutput. On this platform every backend upstream ships is gated
      off -- ALSA is Linux, mac/win are the wrong OS, OSS wants `UNIX` (which
      CMake's Platform/WASI.cmake does not set), FluidSynth/Sonivox/PulseAudio
      are absent, and the Network one is QUdpSocket over multicast -- so
      drumstick builds ZERO backends and BackendManager finds nothing. These
      two are what a wk node actually has: one inbox and one outbox, wired to
      other nodes by drawing a "midi" connection on the canvas.

      THE INTERESTING PART IS THE INPUT PUMP, and it is the constraint that
      shaped the whole port. Every other Drumstick input backend either blocks
      a QThread on a device (alsa-in) or hangs a QSocketNotifier off a
      descriptor (net-in, oss-in). Neither exists here: this Qt is built
      FEATURE_thread=OFF, and `wk:midi/midi` has no descriptor and no pollable
      -- `input.receive()` is a non-blocking pop off a host-side queue. So
      wk-in polls, on a 1 ms Qt::PreciseTimer, which is not the "paper over it
      with a QTimer" anti-pattern plugins/qt/PORTING.md forbids: that rule is
      about a descriptor that HAS a pollable and wants a notifier. Polling is
      the only mechanism this interface offers, it is what every existing wk
      MIDI guest does (plugins/fluidsynth, arp, synth), and the timer only
      shortens a poll QWkEventDispatcher was making anyway -- it already folds
      m_timers.timerWait() into the frame wait. WK_MIDI_POLL_MS overrides it.

      The bytes go straight into drumstick's own rt-backends/common/
      midiparser.cpp, which is COMPILED IN exactly as net-in and oss-in compile
      it in. That parser emits all ten MIDIInput signals and implements
      MIDI-thru, so these backends contain no MIDI parsing whatsoever.

      wk-common exists because wk-in and wk-out are two plugins over ONE pair
      of wk:midi resources, which the shim holds in file statics: compiling the
      shim into both archives would both duplicate its symbols at link time and
      give each plugin its own half-opened resource.

      The BackendManager hunk is the difference between working and qFatal.
      Q_OS_WASI is granted Q_OS_UNIX by fallthrough (plugins/qt/patches/
      qtbase-0002-wasi-system-detection.patch), so without a wk arm placed
      BEFORE it the defaults are {"FluidSynth","OSS"} -- neither of which is in
      this binary -- and VPiano::initialize() dies at "Unable to find a
      suitable output backend."
UPSTREAM: not-as-is, and that is the right answer rather than a hedge: a
      backend for one specific runtime belongs upstream only if that runtime
      is something upstream wants to carry. The SHAPE, though, is entirely
      theirs -- these files are net-in/net-out with Qt::Network swapped out,
      down to the QT_STATICPLUGIN arm and the moc -I. Nothing in drumstick had
      to change to accept them except the two dispatch points above.
diff --git a/library/rt-backends/CMakeLists.txt b/library/rt-backends/CMakeLists.txt
index c0853fa..fb49045 100644
--- a/library/rt-backends/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/library/rt-backends/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -56,3 +56,28 @@ endif()
 if(HAVE_FLUIDSYNTH)
     add_subdirectory(fluidsynth)
 endif()
+
+# --- wk ---------------------------------------------------------------------
+# MIDI in and out over the wk runtime's `wk:midi/midi` port -- a wk node's one
+# MIDI inbox and outbox, wired to other nodes by drawing a "midi" connection on
+# the canvas. wk-common holds the shim both halves share.
+#
+# On this platform every gate above is false: CMake's Platform/WASI.cmake sets
+# only WASI, so UNIX is empty and the OSS arm is skipped; CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME is
+# neither Darwin nor Windows; and ALSA/PulseAudio/Sonivox/FluidSynth/Network
+# are all off for a wasm32-wasip2 build. So drumstick would otherwise build
+# ZERO backends and BackendManager would find nothing at all.
+# STREQUAL, and NOT the `if(${CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME} MATCHES ...)` form the Darwin
+# and Windows arms above use. That is a trap, not a style preference:
+# `${CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME}` expands to the bare word WASI, CMake treats an
+# unquoted MATCHES operand as a VARIABLE NAME when one exists, and
+# Platform/WASI.cmake defines `set(WASI 1)` -- so the test silently becomes
+# `if(1 MATCHES "WASI")`, which is false. The backends are then skipped with no
+# warning at all, the app still links, and the first sign of trouble is
+# qFatal("Unable to find a suitable input backend") at runtime. Darwin and
+# Windows are safe only because no variable of those names happens to exist.
+if(CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME STREQUAL "WASI")
+    add_subdirectory(wk-common)
+    add_subdirectory(wk-in)
+    add_subdirectory(wk-out)
+endif()
diff --git a/library/rt-backends/wk-common/CMakeLists.txt b/library/rt-backends/wk-common/CMakeLists.txt
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..e924b16
--- /dev/null
+++ b/library/rt-backends/wk-common/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -0,0 +1,43 @@
+# The wk:midi shim, compiled ONCE for both wk backends.
+#
+# wk-in and wk-out are two Drumstick plugins over ONE node port pair, and the
+# shim (plugins/qt-drumstickvpiano2111/shim/wkmidiio.c plus the wit-bindgen
+# output beside it) keeps the wk:midi input and output resources in the same
+# file-static struct. Compiling those sources into both plugin archives would
+# therefore be wrong twice over: the linker would see wkmidi_send defined in
+# two objects that are both pulled in, and even if it did not, the two copies
+# would each hold their own half-opened resource.
+#
+# Hence a third target. It is deliberately NOT installed or exported: it is
+# wk's, not drumstick's, and the wk build never runs `cmake --install` on this
+# tree -- the app is linked from the same build directory.
+#
+#   WK_MIDI_SHIM_SRCS      shim/wkmidiio.c + gen/wkmidi.c (build.sh regenerates
+#                          the latter from ../midi-compat/wit every build)
+#   WK_MIDI_SHIM_INCLUDES  the directories holding wkmidiio.h and the generated
+#                          wkmidi.h
+#
+# Note what is NOT here: gen/wkmidi_component_type.o. That object carries only
+# a `component-type` custom section and nothing references it, so as an archive
+# member the linker drops it and the component ends up with no wk:midi import
+# at all. It has to reach the final link as a LINK OPTION -- utils/vpiano does
+# that, via WK_MIDI_COMPONENT_TYPE.
+# Drumstick's project() declares `LANGUAGES CXX`, so a target made only of .c
+# files leaves CMake with nothing to link with: "CMake can not determine linker
+# language for target". Enabling C here rather than editing the top-level
+# project() keeps the change inside the wk subtree. Compiling these two files
+# as C++ instead would be the wrong fix -- the generated bindings use C
+# compound literals.
+enable_language(C)
+
+set(WK_MIDI_SHIM_SRCS "" CACHE STRING "C sources of the wk:midi shim + its wit-bindgen output")
+set(WK_MIDI_SHIM_INCLUDES "" CACHE STRING "include dirs for wkmidiio.h and the generated wkmidi.h")
+
+if(NOT WK_MIDI_SHIM_SRCS)
+    message(FATAL_ERROR "wk backends need -DWK_MIDI_SHIM_SRCS=... (see plugins/qt-drumstickvpiano2111/build.sh)")
+endif()
+
+add_library(drumstick-rt-wk-shim STATIC ${WK_MIDI_SHIM_SRCS})
+
+# PUBLIC: wk-in and wk-out include wkmidiio.h themselves.
+target_include_directories(drumstick-rt-wk-shim PUBLIC ${WK_MIDI_SHIM_INCLUDES})
